/**
 * Values as bytes: encode turns a value into a Binary, decode reads one back
 * at the type the call site is used at.
 *
 * Both are type-directed. The compiler knows the concrete type at every call
 * and emits a descriptor of it into the program, so decode never infers
 * anything from the bytes: Ok(x) is a value of exactly the type asked for, and
 * bad bytes are a DecodeError, never a wrong-shaped value and never a crash.
 *
 * That is also why encode is total and returns a plain Binary rather than a
 * Result: every value is encodable. The one refusal the ruling below leaves
 * standing is about inference, not about a value - a type variable at
 * `decode` with nothing to fix it is answered with "annotate the binding",
 * because the descriptor is a function of the static type and a polymorphic
 * one has none.
 *
 * Decided 2026-08-22 (owner): "Beam allows for everything to be encoded.
 * There is nothing that shouldn't be." A closure encodes as the identity of
 * the run that made it, its function index and its captures, each capture
 * described inline. Pid, Subject, Connection, TlsConnection and net.Server
 * encode as an identity - run, kind, number - that only the run that minted
 * it can use: a foreign one decodes and compares, and fails at USE, the way
 * a pid from another node does on BEAM. An opaque type from another module
 * encodes and decodes by its constructors, and its invariants are that
 * module's to re-check on the values it is handed. Issue #35 was closed on
 * the same ruling.
 *
 * Implementation status at this commit: the opaque rule is in force - an
 * opaque type crosses whenever its fields do, and the visibility refusal is
 * gone. Closures and the five host-backed handles are NOT YET encodable: the
 * compiler still refuses them at the call, staged behind a run identity, a
 * handle node and a capture sub-format that do not exist yet. Not yet is the
 * whole of it: none of them is refused because it never will be.
 *
 * Bytes are tied to a type's shape, not to its name. Two programs that declare
 * the same shape independently interoperate, and any change to the shape is a
 * SchemaMismatch rather than a silent misread.
 *
 * # The fingerprint
 *
 * "The same shape" is decided by a 64-bit fingerprint carried in every encoded
 * value and reported by SchemaMismatch. It is a compatibility surface between
 * separately compiled programs, so it is specified here rather than left to
 * the implementation: a second implementation that follows this section
 * produces the same numbers.
 *
 * The compiler describes a type as a finite table of nodes, each node a scalar
 * (Int, Float, String, Binary), a container (Array, Map, Tuple) or a Data node
 * listing the constructors. A child is an index into that table, which is how a
 * recursive type is finite: List(Int)'s Cons.tail is the index of the List(Int)
 * node itself. The table is laid out by a walk from the root, and the
 * fingerprint hashes it in index order, so nothing recurses.
 *
 * The hash is FNV-1a over 64-bit words:
 *
 *   mix(h, v) = (h XOR v) * 0x100000001b3, mod 2^64
 *
 * starting from h = mix(0xcbf29ce484222325, VERSION), where VERSION is 1.
 * Then mix in the node count, then the root's index, then each node in index
 * order. A name is mixed as its length followed by its UTF-8 bytes, one byte
 * per mix - length first, so ('ab', 'c') and ('a', 'bc') do not collide.
 *
 * Each node mixes a kind tag first: Int 1, Float 2, String 3, Binary 4,
 * Array 5 then the element's index, Map 6 then key and value indices, Tuple 7
 * then the element count and each index, Data 8 then the constructor count and,
 * per constructor in declared order: its tag, its name, its field count, and
 * per field the label and the field type's index.
 *
 * INCLUDED, so each of these is a schema change: node kinds, the order of
 * constructors, constructor names, field labels, and field types.
 *
 * EXCLUDED, so none of these is: the type's own name, and the module it is
 * declared in. Renaming a type or moving it between modules leaves every peer
 * talking, and two programs that declare the same shape under different names
 * agree without ever having shared a declaration.
 *
 * CHANGING THIS ALGORITHM MEANS BUMPING THE FORMAT VERSION BYTE, and bumping
 * VERSION above with it - the same byte "The byte format" below reads. The
 * fingerprint travels in every message: an algorithm change that left the
 * version alone would surface at a peer as a SchemaMismatch on a type nobody
 * had touched.
 *
 * # The byte format
 *
 * Little-endian throughout, and no per-value type tags: the descriptor drives
 * structure on both sides, so the bytes hold only what the descriptor cannot
 * already supply. A second implementation that follows this section produces
 * the same bytes, same bar as the fingerprint above.
 *
 *   value := magic "SW" . version u8 . fingerprint u64 . body
 *
 * `version` is VERSION from the fingerprint algorithm above - one byte, one
 * definition, read by both sections. `fingerprint` is the 64-bit hash of the
 * root type, computed as specified there. A magic other than "SW", or a
 * version this runtime does not read, is NotWire; a fingerprint that parses
 * but disagrees with the reader's own is SchemaMismatch(expected, found) -
 * both sides carried, so a log line can say which end changed.
 *
 * `body` holds the value, laid out by the root node's kind and then, for a
 * container or Data node, recursively by each child's:
 *
 *   Int     zigzag LEB128                  (Int is 64-bit; nothing outside i64 exists)
 *   Float   8 bytes, f64 bits
 *   String  len LEB128, then that many UTF-8 bytes
 *   Binary  bit_len LEB128, then ceil(bit_len / 8) bytes (bit-level binaries round-trip)
 *   Array   count LEB128, then that many elements
 *   Map     count LEB128, then that many key/value pairs
 *   Tuple   its elements, in order (arity comes from the descriptor, not the bytes)
 *   Data    variant LEB128, then that constructor's fields - the variant is
 *           omitted entirely when the type has exactly one, so a record costs
 *           only its fields and Nil is zero bytes
 *
 * A count is checked against the bytes actually remaining before anything is
 * allocated - a short input claiming a huge count is Malformed(offset, what),
 * never an allocation. A complete value followed by more bytes is
 * TrailingBytes(count), reported rather than ignored, because a caller that
 * framed its own messages has lost sync. Input shorter than the value it
 * claims to hold is Truncated.
 */

// A value of any encodable type as bytes. Total: a type this cannot encode
// is rejected when the program is compiled.
@vm(wire__encode)
pub fn encode(value a) Binary

// The value `bytes` holds, as the type this call is used at. The type must
// be known where `decode` is called; `Ok` is a well-formed value of it.
@vm(wire__decode)
pub fn decode(bytes Binary) Result(a, DecodeError)

pub type DecodeError {
	// Fewer bytes than the value needs.
	Truncated
	// Not produced by `wire.encode` (bad magic), or by a format version this
	// runtime does not read.
	NotWire
	// Encoded from a type of a different shape. Both fingerprints, so a log
	// line can say which side changed.
	SchemaMismatch(expected Int, found Int)
	// Well-framed bytes that do not hold a value of the type: a variant tag
	// out of range, a string that is not UTF-8, a count larger than the
	// remaining input. `offset` is the byte it went wrong at.
	Malformed(offset Int, what String)
	// A complete value followed by more bytes. Reported rather than ignored,
	// because a caller that framed its own messages has lost sync.
	TrailingBytes(count Int)
}
